  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "S1",
      "title": "Configure permissions: rules evaluated deny, then ask, then allow; an allow rule can't carve an exception out of a deny rule; permission rules are enforced by Claude Code, not by the model; Read deny rule Read(./.env) also blocks Edit and Write on the same path; applies to cat, head, tail and redirections, not to scripts that open files themselves (sandbox for OS-level enforcement); project settings at .claude/settings.json; /permissions; a tool denied at any level can't be allowed by --allowedTools",
      "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions",
      "checked_at": "2026-11-01",
      "label": "Claude Code docs, permissions"
    },
    {
      "id": "S2",
      "title": "CLI reference: --allowedTools (tools that execute without prompting for permission); --disallowedTools (deny rules)",
      "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference",
      "checked_at": "2026-11-01",
      "label": "Claude Code docs, CLI"
    }
  ],
  "unverified": [
    "Exact wording of the block message in the terminal: not documented; the line 'blocked: Read(./.env)' is an animated view, not Claude Code's original text.",
    "That --allowedTools only applies to one run (calendar angle): not stated like that in the sources; the video only says 'at launch' and that a deny rule still wins (permissions: If a tool is denied at any level, no other level can allow it).",
    "Mods: per the permissions page, without managed settings or a Team/Enterprise login a mod can approve a call a deny rule refuses; not covered in the video, hence no 'always' and no promise.",
    "The sandbox is only mentioned as a pointer (OS level); details live on the sandboxing page, which isn't a calendar source."
